\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Gaussian normalization}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{Q} be a \vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004} on
#{X=\mathbb R^V}, where #{n=|V|\geq 1}, with the specified product
Lebesgue measure. Its \newvocab{Gaussian partition function} is initially
the extended nonnegative integral
##{Z_Q=\int_X\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}\phi^TQ\phi\right)d\phi.}
It satisfies
##{0<Z_Q=\frac{(2\pi)^{n/2}}{\sqrt{\det Q}}<\infty.}
Consequently the \newvocab{Gaussian probability law} with precision
parameter #{Q} is the Borel probability measure
##{\mu_Q(A)=\frac{1}{Z_Q}\int_A
   \exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}\phi^TQ\phi\right)d\phi.}
The change-of-variables argument below adapts the positive Gaussian density
calculation in \citet{Lemma B.54 and Proposition B.55, pp. 504-505}{friedli2017statistical}
to a precision parameter.}

\proof{
\p{First let #{a>0} and #{g_a(x)=\exp(-a x^2/2)}. On #{[-1,1]} this
function is at most one. For #{|x|\geq 1}, the inequality
#{x^2\geq|x|} gives #{g_a(x)\leq\exp(-a|x|/2)}, whose integral over
the real line is #{4/a}. Thus #{I(a)=\int_{\mathbb R}g_a(x)\,dx}
is finite. It is positive because #{g_a(x)\geq\exp(-a/2)>0} on
#{[-1,1]}.}

\p{The real symmetric spectral theorem gives an orthogonal matrix #{U}
with
##{Q=U\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)U^T.}
Every eigenvalue is positive: #{\lambda_i=(Ue_i)^TQ(Ue_i)>0} for the
#{i}-th coordinate vector #{e_i}. Substitute #{\phi=Uy}. Orthogonality
gives #{|\det U|=1}, so product Lebesgue measure is preserved and
##{\begin{aligned}
&\exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}\phi^TQ\phi\right)\\
&\quad=\prod_{i=1}^n\exp(-\lambda_i y_i^2/2).
\end{aligned}}
Tonelli's theorem applies to this nonnegative integrand. Repeated
integration yields #{Z_Q=\prod_{i=1}^n I(\lambda_i)}, a finite positive
product. No covariance identity enters this argument.}

\p{For the value, put #{J=I(1)}. Tonelli and polar coordinates give
##{\begin{aligned}
J^2&=\int_{\mathbb R^2}g_1(x)g_1(y)\,dx\,dy\\
&=2\pi\int_0^\infty r\exp(-r^2/2)\,dr\\
&=2\pi.
\end{aligned}}
Since #{J>0}, the substitution #{u=\sqrt a\,x} gives
#{I(a)=\sqrt{2\pi /a}}. Also #{\det Q=\prod_i\lambda_i>0}, proving
the stated formula for #{Z_Q}. The nonnegative density #{w_Q/Z_Q}
defines a countably additive measure with total mass one.}
}

\p{The reference measure matters for the unnormalized integral. Replacing
#{d\phi} by #{c\,d\phi}, with #{c>0}, multiplies #{Z_Q} by #{c} but
leaves #{\mu_Q} unchanged. A finite-sum
\vocabk{partition function}{ftip-008J} has the same normalizing role;
its finite-support argument does not prove integrability on #{X}.}

\p{Positivity is essential. If #{Q=0}, the weight is one and
#{Z_Q=\infty}, since #{\mathbb R^n} has infinite Lebesgue volume.
For #{Q=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)}, the weight is
#{\exp(-x^2/2)\exp(y^2/2)}. On #{|x|\leq 1} its first factor is
at least #{\exp(-1/2)}, and the integral over #{y} diverges. Neither
case defines the probability law above. A probability law supported on a
proper subspace would require a different reference measure.}

\p{A zero normalizer is a different failure. For a general nonnegative
weight #{w\equiv 0}, one has #{\int_Xw\,d\phi=0} and cannot normalize
by division. This never occurs for the positive Gaussian weight just
proved integrable. In particular, zero precision does not mean zero
partition function.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{integrable_exp_neg_mul_sq} proves the
one-dimensional integrability step for a positive coefficient, and
\leanref{integral_gaussian} computes its integral. The latter is a
value theorem for a totalized integral; the positive-coefficient
integrability hypothesis is necessary for its use here.}
